<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deutsche Handels- und Plantagen-Gesellschaft</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1878</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as DHPG</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Deutsche Handels- und Plantagen-Gesellschaft der Südsee-Inseln zu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> (DHPG) was a German trading and plantation company founded in 1878 to take over the Pacific business of the Hamburg firm <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-c-godeffroy-and-sohn">J. C. Godeffroy &amp; Sohn</a>. It owned large coconut and cocoa plantations in Samoa, worked by labourers recruited from Melanesia and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and dominated the islands' economy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> confiscated its Samoan estates after 1914.</p></div>
